Some jobs need ARM runners, and the hardware-in-the-loop lab needs agents we run ourselves.

# Comparison

table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,yes,yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,yes,yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,**9.6**,via our own agents,yes (required),"$2,150 + our compute"

# Benchmarks

data bench = pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9
chart bar bench x=pipeline y=GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite unit=min title="Median build time by reference pipeline"

Buildkite is fastest on firmware-build and cloud-api-tests. CircleCI edges it on docs-site, where every provider finishes in about 3 minutes.

# Pros and cons

### GitHub Actions
- Pro: hosted ARM runners and self-hosted agents.
- Pro: lives next to our code and pull requests.
- Con: slowest median build at 14.2 minutes.

### CircleCI
- Pro: hosted ARM runners.
- Pro: faster than GitHub Actions on every pipeline.
- Con: most expensive at $3,420 a month.

### Buildkite
- Pro: fastest median build at 9.6 minutes.
- Pro: lowest license cost.
- Con: agents are required, so we pay for our own compute.
- Con: we patch and run the runners.

# Recommendation

! Choose **Buildkite**. The hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway, so Buildkite's agent model costs us little extra.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call, estimated at 6 hours a month.
